WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.240
In this painting there is a bird&nbsp;
on ice skates delivering a letter.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:09.280
If we move on to this other one, we find&nbsp;
an egg with legs, pierced by an arrow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.880
staring at us with a single eye&nbsp;
through a crack in its shell.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.680
And if we venture into the hell of this last one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:21.280
a bird-headed demon devours entire people&nbsp;
while expelling them inside blue bubbles.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.400
All of these creatures were painted more than&nbsp;
five hundred years ago by Hieronymus Bosch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.760
and although at first glance they might seem like&nbsp;
creatures without any meaning, art historians have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:37.200
spent more than five centuries wondering what&nbsp;
these strange monsters could possibly mean.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:43.600
For centuries, popular imagination has painted&nbsp;
Hieronymus Bosch as a madman. For a long time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:49.520
he was seen as a tormented eccentric who painted&nbsp;
nightmares. But as time has passed and his life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.960
has become a little better known, history has&nbsp;
revealed that Bosch was not a madman at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.520
but quite the opposite, as he was a&nbsp;
respected figure in his time. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.480
we could define him as a religious,&nbsp;
prosperous, and perfectly sane man.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.840
The art critic Rachel Campbell-Johnson,&nbsp;
in my opinion, defines him perfectly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:15.120
She says that Bosch's imagination is deeply&nbsp;
rooted in the vocabulary of the medieval world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.360
but that what he does is carry it further, until&nbsp;
it becomes strikingly modern. And she calls him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.440
directly, the first artist who&nbsp;
truly painted the imagination.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.640
On one of his sketches there is a written&nbsp;
phrase that sums up his entire philosophy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.240
"Poor is the mind that always uses the inventions&nbsp;
of others, and invents nothing itself."

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.360
And with that declaration, I&nbsp;
would like us to take a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:43.920
at some of the bizarre creatures&nbsp;
he included in his paintings.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.200
In "The Temptation of Saint Anthony", while the&nbsp;
exhausted saint is being lifted from the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.800
a small creature casually passes through the&nbsp;
scene. This figure has the body of a bird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.560
the ears of a dog, a funnel on its head, and&nbsp;
wears ice skates while delivering a letter.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.520
According to his biographer Athanasius, Saint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.880
Anthony was tormented by all kinds of beasts&nbsp;
in his confrontation with the devil: wolves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:15.200
lions, snakes, and scorpions. But nowhere is&nbsp;
there any mention of a bird-dog on skates.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.560
This makes us wonder: where did Bosch get&nbsp;
this creature? And what meaning could it have?

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.880
Although nobody knows for certain, there&nbsp;
is a fascinating interpretation proposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.680
by Dirk Bax, one of the greatest Bosch&nbsp;
specialists of the twentieth century.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.160
According to Bax, this creature is&nbsp;
a demonic messenger. The badge it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.800
wears on its chest was the emblem that&nbsp;
identified messengers of the era. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.800
the letter it carries would be an&nbsp;
accusation against Saint Anthony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.600
a formal complaint from the demons&nbsp;
protesting the treatment the saint received.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.160
But there is one detail that reveals the&nbsp;
true intention of the scene. The letter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.080
is written in mirror writing,&nbsp;
meaning backwards. And for Bax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.680
that is proof that it is all&nbsp;
a mockery. The demons are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.600
filing a real complaint. They are ridiculing&nbsp;
the saint with false, illegible documents.

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.280
Even the skates would have their&nbsp;
explanation. In Bosch's time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.520
the metaphor of "skating on thin&nbsp;
ice" already existed as a reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.680
to sin. A world gliding over a fragile&nbsp;
surface, about to crack at any moment.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.400
The next creature is not an impossible&nbsp;
hybrid like many of his other monsters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:30.240
This is a completely real animal, and perhaps&nbsp;
that is why it is one of the most unsettling.

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.560
In The Ship of Fools, Bosch painted&nbsp;
a vessel full of drunken revelers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.160
singing and eating without restraint. A scene&nbsp;
of unrestrained celebration. But if we look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:45.600
up toward the tree that serves as the mast,&nbsp;
we find an owl watching everything calmly.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.880
Owls appear constantly in&nbsp;
Bosch's paintings. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.680
everywhere. And they do not mean what we think.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.080
The curator of the National Gallery,&nbsp;
Dr. Leila Packer, explains that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:02.640
owl is an ominous symbol warning us that something&nbsp;
is amiss in paradise. We, in the modern world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.560
see the owl as a symbol of wisdom. But for the&nbsp;
medieval world, that animal with enormous eyes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:15.440
a twisting head was an all-seeing being, sitting&nbsp;
on high, judging. A symbol of menace and caution.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:21.840
And that bad reputation had very deep roots. In&nbsp;
the mindset of the era, the owl was a nocturnal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.600
bird that could see in the darkness but avoided&nbsp;
the light. For that reason it was associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.280
with sinners, with those who turn their backs on&nbsp;
God and prefer to live in the shadows. Medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.160
bestiaries went even further, describing&nbsp;
the owl as an animal that attracted its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:44.400
prey through deception. A perfect metaphor&nbsp;
for the devil tempting unsuspecting souls.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.800
If you want to explore this symbolism in depth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.000
we already made a complete video analyzing&nbsp;
all the owls hidden in Bosch's works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.040
from The Garden of Earthly Delights to The&nbsp;
Haywain. I will leave it in the description.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.720
But for this scene, the essential thing to keep&nbsp;
in mind is that in a boat full of fools who eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.360
drink, and sing without a thought for&nbsp;
their salvation, Bosch placed the symbol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.680
of evil at the highest point of the painting,&nbsp;
watching over the fools as they celebrate.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.360
The next creature is found in the central panel&nbsp;
of The Last Judgment, in the middle of the chaos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.800
In the lower part of the composition we find an&nbsp;
egg with human legs, pierced by an arrow. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.080
through a crack in its shell, an eye peers&nbsp;
out, as if horrified by what it is seeing.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.560
Eggs, just like owls, are everywhere in Bosch's&nbsp;
work. And just like them, they carry a double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:43.040
meaning. They are a symbol of birth, but also of&nbsp;
confinement. Life and prison in a single shell.&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.720
But there is one egg in Bosch's&nbsp;
work that hides something more.

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:51.840
The art critic Kelly Grovier, whom we have&nbsp;
already cited in other videos on this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.880
discovered something extraordinary in The&nbsp;
Garden of Earthly Delights. If we trace an X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.200
from the four corners of the central panel,&nbsp;
at the exact point where the lines cross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.080
at the geometric center of all that&nbsp;
chaos, there is a small unhatched egg.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:10.800
And his interpretation is beautiful.&nbsp;
According to Grovier, through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:16.160
timeless symbol of the unhatched egg, Bosch&nbsp;
offers us a way out of his troubled work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:21.520
since this egg could be interpreted as the&nbsp;
hope of a birth that is always about to come.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:25.520
And finally, in this same&nbsp;
painting but on its right panel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.280
we arrive at the most terrifying creature of all.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.440
But before talking about it, I would like&nbsp;
to remind you that if you would like to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.320
any of these paintings in your collection,&nbsp;
you can now purchase them from our catalog.

