00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:02.000
In the hell panel of The Garden of Earthly&nbsp;
Delights, a bird-headed demon sits on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:08.000
a latrine. In its hands it holds one of the&nbsp;
damned, whom it devours slowly. And below it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:13.520
the already consumed bodies are expelled&nbsp;
inside blue bubbles into a dark pit.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.800
Historians know this creature as the Prince&nbsp;
of Hell. And every detail of its figure was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.760
designed to invert the order of things.&nbsp;
It wears a cooking cauldron as a crown.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:28.800
Its feet are tucked into two jugs, as if&nbsp;
they were boots. And its throne, the place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.804
from which it rules all of hell, is a chair&nbsp;
that at the same time functions as a latrine.

00:07:33.804 --> 00:07:33.856
It is the perfect image to&nbsp;
represent Bosch's style,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.856 --> 00:07:33.920
since in a way he takes the&nbsp;
everyday and completely perverts it.

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.560
Art historians explain this scene as&nbsp;
follows: in this fragment of the work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:44.160
Bosch plays with our perception of scale and&nbsp;
inverts our understanding of the world. Everyday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:50.640
objects are enlarged until they become industrial&nbsp;
machines that consume humans. A harp becomes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:55.120
gallows. A meat grinder processes human flesh, and&nbsp;
even gaming tables appear perverted toward evil.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:59.440
But the most disturbing thing about this&nbsp;
creature is the cycle it represents.

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:05.840
The demon devours the damned and expels them.&nbsp;
It eats and excretes, over and over again, in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:12.640
grotesque cycle that has no end. And according to&nbsp;
filmmaker David Bickerstaff, that is precisely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:19.680
pattern repeated throughout Bosch's universe: a&nbsp;
constant circle of birth, consumption, and death.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:25.840
But there is one last creature we must talk about.&nbsp;
And in my opinion, it is the most personal of all.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:31.040
In Saint John the Evangelist on Patmos, while&nbsp;
the saint writes the Book of Revelation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:33.920
a small insectoid creature accompanies him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:39.040
a corner. It has a cauldron on its&nbsp;
head and arrows stuck in its belly.

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:44.480
This mysterious creature is believed to&nbsp;
possibly be a self-portrait of Bosch himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.280
and although there is no direct evidence of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.280
there are several details that&nbsp;
have led historians to consider it.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.280
The first of them is its appearance.&nbsp;
As we have seen throughout this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:00.400
his monsters usually do not have human&nbsp;
faces. On the contrary, the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:06.880
have an animal-like appearance. However, this&nbsp;
particular figure has a completely human face.

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:11.360
Another detail worth keeping in mind is&nbsp;
that this work was probably created for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.320
the chapel of the Brotherhood of Our Lady&nbsp;
in the cathedral of his city. A religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:22.080
confraternity of which Bosch himself was a&nbsp;
member. So it was a deeply personal work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.200
destined for a place where he himself prayed.&nbsp;
Additionally, the creature is in the lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:33.680
right corner of the painting. Exactly the place&nbsp;
where artists signed their works. And curiously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:38.880
this painting is believed to be one of the&nbsp;
first to carry Bosch's characteristic signature.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:43.680
We should also remember that medieval and&nbsp;
Renaissance artists often hid their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.800
portraits in their works, something we have&nbsp;
already seen many times on this channel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:54.240
So although the theory that this strange creature&nbsp;
is a self-portrait of Bosch is not confirmed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.920
and probably never will be, it&nbsp;
is fascinating to think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:04.000
an artist like Bosch decided to include&nbsp;
himself in one of his works in this way.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:06.800
I would love for you to leave&nbsp;
me in the comments which of all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.280
the creatures we saw in this video&nbsp;
you find most disturbing, and why.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:13.760
I will be reading your answers in the comments.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:18.080
Thank you for watching the video until the&nbsp;
end, and I will see you in the next video.

